   I am writing a program for the Mac II that does software synthesis like Csound. It contains a yacc based compiler and an interpreter that allows one to create patches like small C programs. It allows you to draw envelopecurves, fill wavetables with sine harmonics or chebyshev polynomials (forwaveshaping transfer functions), and view the generated sample. All operations are performed in double precision floating point and stored at the end as floats. It is resonably fast and quite powerful. As an example of the look of the code and the speed, the following program when generating51,200 samples executes in 19.6 seconds on a plain Mac II :// 100 Hz sine phase modulated with an index of 9 by another sine wave, // c:m ratio = 1:1a = oscil(sinetbl, 100, 9 * oscil(sinetbl, 100, 0));// control signal for Butterworth bandpass filter center freq (in cps) :b = 500 * (1.1 + oscil(tbl1, .4, .7));// control signal for Butterworth lowpass filter center freq (in cps) :c = 500 * (1.1 + oscil(tbl1, .3, 2.2));// filter signal 'a' twice and then balance it with unfiltered version :out = balance(lpf(bpf(a, b, 50), c), a);  The program contains equivalents to most of the unit generators in Csound plus some of my own.	I intend on releasing this program free of charge along with the source code. WHAT I WANT IS FOR YOU TO SEND ME SOURCE CODE OR THE SYS-EX CODES FOR TRANSFERRING SAMPLES TO YOUR FAVORITE SAMPLER SO I CAN SUPPORT AS MANY SAMPLERS AS POSSIBLE.If you send me source code or sys-ex documentation I will make sure you are one of the first to receive a copy of the program. Also documentation on any file formats would also be useful. I've already got it creating SoundDesigner files. Anyone know the Alchemy file format? I also need to find outthe AIFF format. Any pointers to this documentation would also be helpful.--- James McCartney	P.S. I assume you could run it on a Mac+ or SE but it would be as slow 	as molasses.	